2026-11-22 14:34:50 - Agent_20261122143212 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 5 py/static-file-access-aiohttp results (aiohttp 3.13.2). All are false positives:

1. aiohttp/web_urldispatcher.py:566 (url_for, `Path(os.path.normpath(unresolved_path))`) — path is derived from `self._directory.joinpath(filename)` where filename comes from developer-supplied `url_for(filename=...)`, not a request; followed by `normalized_path.relative_to(self._directory)` and `filepath.relative_to(self._directory)` guards.
2. aiohttp/web_urldispatcher.py:611/619/622 (url_for with follow_symlinks) — both branches perform `relative_to(self._directory)` containment checks inside try/except (ValueError, FileNotFoundError) returning early, and `_follow_symlinks` resolution ordering is correct (normpath+relative_to before resolve for symlink mode; resolve+relative_to otherwise). No request-controlled input.
3. aiohttp/web_fileresponse.py:233 (`fobj = file_path.open("rb")`) — `self._path` is set in `FileResponse.__init__` from a developer-supplied path; when used through StaticResource._resolve_path_to_response it has already been validated by `resolve()` + `relative_to(self._directory)`.

Static path traversal defense is also present in `StaticResource._handle`: `filename.anchor` check rejects absolute paths/UNC (`/static/\\machine\c$`, `/static/D:\path`), and `_resolve_path_to_response` raises HTTPNotFound on any path escaping the root directory. `_unquote_path_safe` only decodes %2F and %25 without allowing legitimate percent-encoded traversal past the post-resolution containment check, which operates on the resolved real path.

No path traversal vulnerability found; all five results classified as false positives.
