2026-11-22 14:36:27 - Agent_20261122143212 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the single CodeQL result for py/pickle-all-aiohttp in aiohttp/cookiejar.py. Investigated the file: `import pickle` (line 9), `CookieJar.save()` (lines 125-128) which uses `pickle.dump`, and `CookieJar.load()` (lines 130-133) which uses `pickle.load(f)` on an attacker-supplied path. Grepped the entire package for callers of `.save(`/`.load(` on cookie jars and for pickle usage elsewhere in library code — found no internal callers; the only sink is the opt-in public API `CookieJar.load(file_path)`, invoked explicitly by the application developer with a path they control. Other pickle usages are confined to tests (test_cookiejar.py, test_client_exceptions.py, test_http_exceptions.py, test_websocket_parser.py) which merely round-trip objects with dumps/loads in-process and are not exploitable. No hidden dataflow from network response cookies (client.py:691 `all_cookies.load(req_cookies)` is a dict-like update, not pickle). Conclusion: the pickle.load in `save`/`load` is an inherently unsafe deserialization primitive only reachable through a developer-initiated API on a local file; exploitation requires the application to load a pickle file that an attacker can write (already implies filesystem compromise). Marked the `save()`/method-definition line as a false positive (outbound serialization only, no deserialization of untrusted data) and the `pickle.load(f)` sink at line 133 as manual-review/low-risk design concern since safety depends entirely on caller-supplied file provenance. No new independent vulnerabilities discovered.
